ture/13931259/assets/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/c9b75fa5f325825788ae2a64e227779c81af2454230ded498d7245dd9e0c66cf.png) be the endpoint of the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/7ab3eab6d396347c576f9565da06cf224deb593d51a3943062d6d96e56761ad4.png) Then\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/2159390e2c15cbecf0750f9c9b061c45b95bec745349cbd7c31e1aea834e8022.png)\n\nIn ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/934e201a8788fc6c8ff3a81d3946b86454ce5a69d0d9d1466cf0fff9a0de6ef5.png) we have:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/597714ab8fa507e93f9e7ca11e7b91a8d752428df879dd5250647b9e54a87853.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/7db8a41266cbcadf83361552751884e082648f2caf1ec786a372cb1e57a7e794.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/4c41b183428cf433289d7ef5bdadd3f83e100e5e4e0feb57d8c3afd647a05696.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/ea63be325f82db32d019c101aff9fd96e6e153ffa86524fd755cb5b1bda8396e.png)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/8388dbaf40f15bb799039154b6e2ed8f5ce8699b1e96ef47af03458d827dd392.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/fd66eeaaabc163ea5004d6e89629ff6e28a310e555301703ab605c1dbd7f8ebb.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/e1d3b43e599ec0e4cb548b65a18315fb0d5fe155dad8b6f1fbabb63c42998c14.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/874fc16afdc829b59ebb3f871e48722f6a11e475577e3195549d68cb2c836d30.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/033debb7dd8e9b9dbd5caa4fbe21557f26979dc19aa16ebd607fa29de34e9f5d.png)\n\nConsequently,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/1b1692325288b047f70aa0aa09921e576c3e61f22ee06b295bc72776a615b223.png)\n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/cdd03b199d81550020678e938a618057ec249985289ab595de8701117ff08e67.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/af7376fd2f2674cbb36c9a0128d1f90bbfc15082cb11fde35ce8b20a28b9043f.png) we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/f9b2860b8a585a97d625d1a0cad6d1373766af5260d731a173850f5d5c4bbc3c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/bfa592bb1f4e5a292e1e94476ab6e5f1.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/49fbbe1f5dd4731054af33666b88cb64.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2 \\mid \\mathbf{n} \\mid$;\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}\n$$\n\n.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2 \\mid \\mathbf{k} \\mid$\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n.\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ON^(→) + NM^(→) \\\\\n&= 2n + 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k}$, the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1$. Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/8512f8ab1660315b7e5f0aa8beb302ff.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nso we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}$.\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= AO^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-OA^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-a + b \\\\\n&= b - a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOX^(→) &= OA^(→) + AX^(→) \\\\\n&= a + \\frac{3}{4}(b - a) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}a + \\frac{3}{4}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/c2827bf97f6d17572e9ac2b808d16a69.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/0b673cb49bd6ea0955e57952763b7458.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup OAC$, we have:\n\n- $AC=2 \\mid \\mathbf{b} \\mid$\n- $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AC}=2\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= OA^(→) + AC^(→) \\\\\n&= a + 2b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b}$, the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b}$, we get:\n$\\lambda=2$"}]}
